 `?result=/run/result_mesh.json` remains supported for a project-local, read-only run preview; loading a local result file is also supported. Demo replay never invokes a model or a solver. The shared viewer renders material points without invented FE elements and distinguishes Damage from displacement. See [Peridigm demo guide](../MCP/Peridigm/DEMO.md).
 
+### Finite-element results / 有限元结果
+
+The same page also renders 8-node hex FE results (`representation` absent, `elements` present), e.g. a
+CalculiX run exported with `MCP/CalculiX/tools/viewer_case.py`. Put the exported `result_mesh.json`
+under `dist/run/` and open:
+
+```text
+/peridigm.html?result=/run/result_mesh.json
+```
+
+Surface extraction keeps only boundary quadrilaterals; a 207k-element mesh collapses to 16,226 faces.
+`viewer/scripts/bench-surface.mjs` measures that step (integer bucket dedupe vs the older
+string-key dedupe: 188 ms vs 661 ms on the same mesh). Nodes may omit `deformed` — the viewer
+recomputes it from `coordinates + deformationScale * displacement`, which cuts the JSON by ~13%.
+
